{"title":"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 4","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_in_3_minutes!!!\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution in 3 minutes!!!</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> be a triangle with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/4/ea494684dc60997279257d5a54e877a1fb28ca60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A = 90^{\\circ}$\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" />. Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />\nand <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> lie on sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, respectively, such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/c/6cc0e04c1b9d3636e9fef80b152f0a149d535e26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABD = \\angle DBC$\" width=\"138\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/7/92795aed81f4bb5f45bd951633bfe47843d98778.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ACE = \\angle ECB$\" width=\"135\" height=\"13\" />. Segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> and\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c970cbf5e2e7029089517d5e8efbb8cade6d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> meet at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.  Determine whether or not it is possible for\nsegments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/6/bf6a00bfbcedc80aaea0eb0c13cadbb4f783036d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB, AC, BI, ID, CI, IE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"192\" height=\"16\" /> to all have integer lengths.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_in_3_minutes.21.21.21\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_in_3_minutes!!!\">Video Solution in 3 minutes!!!</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution in 3 minutes!!!\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z9fjesTOs1Q\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z9fjesTOs1Q</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We know that angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/d/a7df01eb20113268dc5fdf9501ad19e18658e330.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BIC = 135^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"99\" height=\"15\" />, as the other two angles in triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/e/9be6bb46eb26ee5d3912a8a9ad18ee37593827b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BIC$\" width=\"38\" height=\"12\" /> add to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/1/821d3f11ab7a3e93c7a404a34224228f0449505a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" />. Assume that only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/5/ab547e71f076d33d55798fcd5536494289faff5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB, AC, BI$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/d/a8d37413bae1973358eebff164711f292d16be56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CI$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"26\" height=\"14\" /> are integers. Using the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Law_of_Cosines\" title=\"Law of Cosines\">Law of Cosines</a> on triangle BIC,\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/b/0cb024c176968ded79ed251e2aafd828e0d71002.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import olympiad;  // Scale unitsize(1inch);  // Shape real h = 1.75; real w = 2.5;  // Points void ldot(pair p, string l, pair dir=p) { dot(p); label(l, p, unit(dir)); } pair A = origin; ldot(A, &quot;$A$&quot;, plain.SW); pair B = w * plain.E; ldot(B, &quot;$B$&quot;, plain.SE); pair C = h * plain.N; ldot(C, &quot;$C$&quot;, plain.NW); pair D = extension(B, bisectorpoint(C, B, A), A, C); ldot(D, &quot;$D$&quot;, D-B); pair E = extension(C, bisectorpoint(A, C, B), A, B); ldot(E, &quot;$E$&quot;, E-C); pair I = extension(B, D, C, E); ldot(I, &quot;$I$&quot;, A-I);  // Segments draw(A--B); draw(B--C); draw(C--A); draw(C--E); draw(B--D);  // Angles import markers; draw(rightanglemark(B, A, C, 4)); markangle(Label(&quot;$\\scriptstyle{\\frac{\\theta}{2}}$&quot;), radius=40, I, B, E); markangle(Label(&quot;$\\scriptstyle{\\frac{\\theta}{2}}$&quot;), radius=40, C, B, I); markangle(Label(&quot;$\\scriptstyle{\\frac{\\pi}{4} - \\frac{\\theta}{2}}$&quot;), radius=40, I, C, B); markangle(Label(&quot;$\\scriptstyle{\\frac{\\pi}{4} - \\frac{\\theta}{2}}$&quot;), radius=40, D, C, I); markangle(Label(&quot;$\\scriptstyle{\\frac{3\\pi}{4}}$&quot;), radius=10, B, I, C); [/asy]\" width=\"348\" height=\"255\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/e/55e9efb89b5f3d112f8fefec275b1ffd16a71411.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC^2 = BI^2 + CI^2 - 2BI\\cdot CI \\cdot \\cos 135^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"311\" height=\"16\" />. Observing that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/2/2924cf560dc5ff4c59e350c197fbabfe134970c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC^2 = AB^2 + AC^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"152\" height=\"16\" /> is an integer and that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a3dcdf2eb0201a52a5818c90da919a95d8f14c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos 135^{\\circ} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"133\" height=\"41\" /> we have\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/9/8e97c01d54439247c9bdcdc1b8dd0adac5a140ed.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[BC^2 - BI^2 - CI^2 = BI\\cdot CI\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\]\" width=\"266\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>and therefore,\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/3/c53dadbc3235d0e9e274bb2867201af53a3b9ff7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{2} = \\frac{BC^2 - BI^2 - CI^2}{BI\\cdot CI}\\]\" width=\"193\" height=\"39\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>The LHS (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/4/2c4dc1409208ff6a3039f4871d85c16763334983.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"26\" height=\"21\" />) is irrational, while the RHS is the quotient of the division of two integers and thus is rational. Clearly, there is a contradiction. Therefore, it is impossible for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/5/ab547e71f076d33d55798fcd5536494289faff5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB, AC, BI$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/d/a8d37413bae1973358eebff164711f292d16be56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CI$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"26\" height=\"14\" /> to all be integers, which invalidates the original claim that all six lengths are integers, and we are done.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The answer is no.\n</p><p>Suppose otherwise. It is easy to see (through simple angle chasing) that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/8/c980808228107d2c2efc09ffdee234d87e42c275.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DIC=45^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"104\" height=\"15\" />. Also, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the incenter, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/e/06ebe711084206cf2934b04d4eb3bde429eb10c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle IAC = 45^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"102\" height=\"15\" />. Using the Law of Cosines, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/a/41acdee12c5a7998e57ce07e7bbb74e2ed2e7c61.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[CD^2=IC^2+ID^2-\\sqrt{2}(IC)(ID),\\]\" width=\"274\" height=\"21\" /> so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> is irrational. But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/2/b82951d2a500cba4364c24288704c66c8f205f11.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle IAC \\sim \\triangle DIC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"135\" height=\"18\" />, thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/5/915a5cc37c80f6acc69e268585d80f443ba91095.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$IC^2=CD\\cdot AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"128\" height=\"17\" />, implying that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> is rational, contradiction. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The result can be also proved without direct appeal to trigonometry,\nvia just the angle bisector theorem and the structure of Pythagorean\ntriples. (This is a lot more work).\n</p><p>A triangle in which all the required lengths are integers exists if and\nonly if there exists a triangle in which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> are\nrelatively-prime integers and the lengths of the segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/e/e7e1b12f6470a00e4d6d66d7746fc7935b655ca9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BI, ID, CI, IE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"120\" height=\"16\" /> are all rational\n(we divide all the lengths by the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/1/ea196f56ed54782fa9d3309ac4cd91575bb1b5c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(AB, AC)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"104\" height=\"18\" /> or\nconversely multiply all the lengths by the least common multiple\nof the denominators of the rational lengths).\n</p><p>Suppose there exists a triangle in which the lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> are\nrelatively-prime integers and the lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/3/aa36453eb34b5985fb774f1efa850e34d6e7dd96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$IB, ID, CI, IE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"123\" height=\"18\" /> are all rational.\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c970cbf5e2e7029089517d5e8efbb8cade6d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is the bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/2/0728bebb477a2636300c56851b6a6f9e382c1b83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ACB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" />, by the angle bisector\ntheorem, the ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/accf9b587d5e7615a7ba78f0f791ded8e8130e23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$IB : ID = CB : CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"164\" height=\"14\" />, and since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> is the\nbisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3ad57710b6810476e6017ea2c2cc6d41219c218.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/c/edc4a7ba1f52f0219e10b6f21065ff8607ea2b10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CB : CD = (AB + BC) : AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"234\" height=\"18\" />. Therefore,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/d/f3d85e86b06ab5e16dee01383bc8661419096b7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$IB : ID = (AB + BC) : AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"225\" height=\"18\" />. Now <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/2/8222bb14b23d4fd9de4f76b1b03c8ef52c2e64ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$IB : ID$\" width=\"63\" height=\"12\" /> is by assumption rational,\nso <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/a/dcaf1cddce5dc4fac302d128c45c7d0fdd0a54ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(AB + BC) : AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"136\" height=\"18\" /> is rational, but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> are assumed integers\nso <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/1/13177e0a3ff53fc2e1b19e742220abeb54dcca08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC : AC$\" width=\"71\" height=\"13\" /> must also be rational. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is the hypotenuse of\na right-triangle, its length is the square root of an integer,\nand thus either an integer or irrational, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> must be an integer.\n</p><p>With <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> relatively-prime, we conclude that the side\nlengths of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> must be a Pythagorean triple: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/9/c99d00de431094cdcbc80587e218a0a511aa4fc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2pq, p^2 - q^2, p^2 + q^2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"166\" height=\"19\" />, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5b6051ac0a556f98f7481e62093ba35754d8805.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p &gt; q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> relatively-prime positive integers\nand <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/0/170200756543596efeb46fca1cf29a617e8c9192.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p+q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"41\" height=\"14\" /> odd.\n</p><p>Without loss of generality, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6a84f5150c49faede92e665e31cf17fb56e364c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC = 2pq, AB = p^2 - q^2, BC = p^2+q^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"313\" height=\"20\" />.\nBy the angle bisector theorem,\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/d/44d99d60751d456d0d88860a431bad1a908017d4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} AE &amp;= \\dfrac{AB \\cdot AC}{AC + CB} = \\dfrac{2pq(p^2-q^2)}{p^2 + q^2 + 2pq}    = \\dfrac{2pq(p-q)}{p+q} \\end{align*}\" width=\"375\" height=\"42\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/c/37c8f83cb958c66bd093ce4ecaaeb511cde6c3c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle CAE$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" /> is a right-triangle, we have:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/f/8afefc25cee4a5cc48030487b1a218e1e5fb040e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}   CE^2 &amp;= AC^2 + AE^2        = 4p^2q^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{2pq(p-q)}{p+q}\\right)^2        = 4p^2q^2\\left[1 + \\left(\\dfrac{p-q}{p+q}\\right)^2\\right] \\\\        &amp;= \\frac{4p^2q^2}{(p+q)^2}\\left[(p+q)^2 + (p-q)^2\\right]        = \\frac{4p^2q^2}{(p+q)^2}(2p^2 + 2q^2) \\end{align*}\" width=\"554\" height=\"104\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>and so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c970cbf5e2e7029089517d5e8efbb8cade6d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is rational if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/8/b989e08976e50dd9ea5bfaac0e93be3e34c3a71b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2p^2 + 2q^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"72\" height=\"18\" /> is a perfect square.\n</p><p>Also by the angle bisector theorem,\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/2/472c79d213b34f035be6f3c8788046ee977ed0b3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} AD &amp;= \\dfrac{AB \\cdot AC}{AB + BC} = \\dfrac{2pq(p^2-q^2)}{p^2 + q^2 + p^2 - q^2}     = \\dfrac{q(p^2-q^2)}{p} \\end{align*}\" width=\"401\" height=\"42\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>and therefore, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/e/8be25cedbf9b65c5d6d74dc50e886682a0778627.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle DAB$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /> is a right-triangle, we have:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/0/770cfc0d4586308ea668633367ba545133831574.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}   BD^2 &amp;= AB^2 + AD^2        = (p^2-q^2)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{q(p^2-q^2)}{p}\\right)^2 \\\\        &amp;= (p^2-q^2)^2\\left[1 + \\frac{q^2}{p^2}\\right]        = \\frac{(p^2-q^2)^2}{p^2}(p^2 + q^2) \\end{align*}\" width=\"393\" height=\"96\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>and so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> is rational if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/1/55159665df27e22b5091eddaeb5e4e7956217e43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^2 + q^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" /> is a perfect square.\n</p><p>Combining the conditions on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c970cbf5e2e7029089517d5e8efbb8cade6d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />, we see that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/8/7d8d7c6a8383591ce431d1ed03d880b419cc7a5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2p^2+2q^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"72\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/d/c5dd8a6e3e95d422c1b9515157464bf5ef9b469e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^2+q^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" /> must both be perfect squares. If it were so,\ntheir ratio, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, would be the square of a rational number,\nbut <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/4/2c4dc1409208ff6a3039f4871d85c16763334983.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"26\" height=\"21\" /> is irrational, and so the assumed triangle cannot exist.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We proceed by contradiction.\n</p><p>FTSOC, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/6/9d60c2b6c7f574009ea12633bbf039591a33121d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB, BI, ID$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" /> have integer lengths. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/7/36769385374f426b7f61bb058358ee7f1a49a2f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD = BI + ID \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"162\" height=\"16\" /> as well. By trigonometry, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/6/0c6b3f81b1a438be941050d66f9a6c60e143fa49.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[BD = \\frac{AB}{\\cos{(\\frac{\\angle ABC}{2})}}.\\]\" width=\"148\" height=\"45\" />\nRearranging we find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/3/f3381e0a74f1101be015d5b38d3117819f5a6cd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos{(\\frac{\\angle ABC}{2})} = \\frac{AB}{BD} \\in \\mathbb{Q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"197\" height=\"39\" />. As <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/d/59d86a11b4969ad4875d83357aeb97134e101fea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 &lt; \\angle ABC &lt; \\frac{\\pi}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"130\" height=\"34\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/0/9606dacec1fb3169fe0e7851befaa60a603a93bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 &lt; \\frac{\\angle ABC}{2} &lt; \\frac{\\pi}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"134\" height=\"39\" /> but there are no possible angles in the interval that result in a rational cosine by Niven's theorem. So we have contradiction.\n</p><p>~Aaryabhatta1.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Dh9H_DNDMAg\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Dh9H_DNDMAg</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO\" title=\"2010 USAMO\">2010 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=2010\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO\" title=\"2010 USAJMO\">2010 USAJMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=176&amp;year=2010\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b>Last Problem</b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_3\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_6\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAJMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAJMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAJMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017223628\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.057 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.084 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 510/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 2272/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 102/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 17089/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   25.435      1 -total\n 44.07%   11.210      1 Template:USAJMO_newbox\n 34.77%    8.844      1 Template:USAMO_newbox\n 19.39%    4.931      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:8685:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20